[{:seon.task/name "definitions-from-the-compiler"
  :seon.task/plan "Lane LOOP (in flight). Each top-level form's definitions come from Jolt's analyzer (jolt.analyzer/*unit-tap*, Jolt 9e42a0d8): vars by kind, methods [multi dispatch], protocol impls [protocol type], the ns form and its requires, resolved refs (vars, macros, classes), arities, exact text; readback after the eval decides what actually ran. Applied per form through the run-ahead writer (225 us, flat; no waiting). Replaces seon.program/def-heads, def-form?, recording's form tests, names-in, ns-env, harvest's flagged guesses and the request-level :defs buffer (repl.clj:228). Design: notes/audit-2026-10-21/program-facts-draft.edn (inv4: 133/133 definitions agree with the runtime on 2,016 real agent forms). Main loads forms in the topological order of the real refs and ns requires."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-form-records-exactly-what-the-compiler-defined
                      seon.program-test/defmethod-extend-and-defrecord-merge-and-load-on-main
                      seon.program-cost-test/recording-a-form-is-flat-in-the-program}}
 {:seon.task/name "program-mutations-are-refused-at-merge"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner 2026-10-21: program code (what merges) does not call clojure.core/intern, alter-var-root, eval or load-string (loading code is an effect), and defines nothing inside a fn body (inline-def). The done rule refuses the branch naming the definition and the call, from the compiler's resolved refs (never text). The REPL still evaluates them; the merge judges. Platform and library code are not judged."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.program/definitions-from-the-compiler"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-program-calling-alter-var-root-is-refused-naming-it
                      seon.program-test/a-def-inside-a-fn-body-is-refused}}
 {:seon.task/name "tests-run-by-reach"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner 2026-10-21: \"I want faster iteration not a matrix with every run permutation\", and \"incremental tests even across worktrees based on test digest\" (AGENTS.md section 3, results are reused by input digest). Gap: `bin/test` (test/test_runner.clj, `test-runner/-main`) ran every deftest each time (266 tests, 5-8 min); `seon.program/reaching-tests` answered which tests reach a definition, but nothing keyed a result by what the test reaches or reused one. v1 (`~/src/seon` seon.test.cache) keyed a result by the test's reach digest and ran only tests with no result; its lesson kept here: inputs without program facts (resources, fixtures, the dependency pins, the launchers) are folded into every digest, never left out. Design, extending what exists: (1) `seon.program/reach-digests` gives each test of a db its reach digest: its own form, its namespace's ns form and non-defining forms, and every live form its `:form/refs` reach transitively (a var to the form that last defined it, a multimethod's methods, a protocol's extensions), each form digested once (`seon.error/digest`), plus the checkout's `:env` digest (every tracked file but src/test .clj, task files and prose: deps.edn, bin/, resources/ with the schemas contracts name, test fixtures, every submodule pin, Jolt's included), plus the whole tree's digest for a test reaching a tree reader (`seon.program/tree-readers`: slurp, file-seq). (2) `bin/test --reach [NS...]` imports src and test as program facts (`seon.source/import-tx`, the same facts root and agents record), computes the digests, reuses every green result stored at its digest and runs the rest, storing each result. (3) Results live in one content-addressed directory shared by every checkout and worktree (`:seon.config.source/test-results-dir`, ~/src/seon-jolt/test-results: one file per digest, written temp-then-rename through `seon.fx/write-file`), not the one store: a checkout's store is runs/store under that checkout, so a store cannot be shared across worktrees. A landing runs `bin/test --reach`: what the change reaches, minus what is green at its digest. A change selects exactly the tests reaching it, since a test's digest moves only when a form it reaches does (`seon.program/reaching-tests` is the inverse closure). Known gaps: a test reading a file by a reader outside `tree-readers` or resolving a var by name at run time is digested without it; importing the program costs about 10 s per run (incremental import by file is the next step)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-changed-function-changes-the-digests-of-exactly-the-tests-reaching-it
                      seon.program-test/a-second-run-with-nothing-changed-runs-nothing
                      seon.program-test/a-result-recorded-in-one-checkout-is-reused-in-another}}
 {:seon.task/name "refs-name-quoted-symbols-and-macro-uses"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the shrink audit 2026-10-22 (notes/shrink-dead-code-2026-10-22.md, 'A gap in the compiler facts'; filed by lane shrink-dead). What exists: `seon.program/unit-account` walks the analyzer's IR and records a form's :refs from :var, :the-var and :host-static nodes only; `seon.program/account` stores them as `:form/refs` (`seon.program/account-tx`), and three readers trust them: `my.agent/callers` (and `my.agent/callees`), `seon.program/reaching-tests` and the reach digests of docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach (task seon.program/tests-run-by-reach). What it misses, so those readers answer wrong: (1) a syntax-quoted symbol, which the reader expands to (quote ns/name) and the analyzer sees as a constant: `seon.render.ui/system-page` names each tile's render syntax-quoted (tile-tasks, resolved later by `requiring-resolve`); (2) a quoted qualified symbol: `'seon.check/probe-proc` in `seon.check/system-check`'s proc map, `'seon.e2e/always-wrong` as `seon.e2e/stuck`'s scripted model, and every :seon.render/html or :malli/schema-declared symbol resolved by name; (3) a macro use: `seon.sock/with-mem` and `seon.config/baked-schema-forms` are expanded before the IR exists, so the forms using them do not name them. Result measured by the audit on main 6114183: `my.agent/callers` reports 25 live `seon.render.ui` functions and about 10 others as having no caller, so an agent deleting by callers breaks the dashboard; a test reaching a function only through a quoted symbol keeps its old digest when that function changes, so a stale green result is reused. Correct: (1) and (2) in `seon.program/unit-account`: a :quote or :const node whose value is a qualified symbol that resolves to a var (or a collection constant holding such symbols, walked) adds that symbol to :refs; an unqualified syntax-quote is already qualified by the reader, so no alias resolution is needed; a symbol that resolves to no var is data, not a ref. (3) the macro: the analyzer has the macro var when it expands the form; record it from the tap (`seon.program/tap`) or ask Jolt's analyzer to keep the macro's var on the expanded node (a dependency change: Jolt, on a jolt/<topic> branch, root's go first); either way the macro's var lands in :refs. What v1 did: v1's seon.ref resolved names in text, not code; v1's callers read the compiler the same way and had the same gap for quoted names (verify in ~/src/seon before porting). Cost: one more case per IR node walked, flat per form (`seon.program-cost-test/recording-a-form-is-flat-in-the-program` must stay green). It replaces nothing and deletes nothing; it makes `my.agent/callers` safe to delete by, after which the brief rule 'confirm with grep across src, test, resources, bin and docs' (notes/lanes/brief-common.md step 4) can go. Wiki: docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach states the refs a digest follows; the landing adds that quoted names and macros are among them. Proofs: a-quoted-or-syntax-quoted-var-is-a-ref: recording (defn g [] 1) then (defn f [] [g-syntax-quoted 'app.pt-q/g {:render 'app.pt-q/g}]) (g-syntax-quoted: g under a syntax quote) in app.pt-q gives f's form :form/refs containing app.pt-q/g, and (defn k [] 'no.such/var) does not add no.such/var. a-macro-use-is-a-ref: (defmacro m [x] x) then (defn u [] (m 1)) gives u's form :form/refs containing app.pt-q/m. a-test-reaching-a-quoted-function-changes-digest-with-it: a deftest calling (requiring-resolve 'app.pt-q/g) changes its reach digest when g is redefined, and `seon.program/reaching-tests` of #{app.pt-q/g} contains it. Done by lane reach (2026-10-22), with the schema edge of notes/fork-test-merge-design-2026-10-22.md section 2 step 2(c): (1)(2) `seon.program/quoted-vars` in `seon.program/unit-account` (a :quote or :const datum walked; a qualified symbol whose var exists); (3) `seon.program/macro-uses` in `seon.program/account`, read from the form's own text in its namespace (each list whose head resolves to a macro var), not from the tap: the tap's unit form of a top-level macro call expanding to a do is the expansion's child, which names no macro, and Jolt's analyzer keeps no macro on the node; no Jolt change was needed. A macro whose template names another reaches it through (1). Schemas: `seon.program/reach-digests` adds each :schema/key entry as a node (its form digested), an edge from a contracted var's form to the entries its contract names and from an entry to those its form names (`seon.program/names`). Clojure's own macros are recorded too (clojure.core/defn on every defn form), as the IR already records clojure.core functions. Measured on src and test (330 tests, 2,236 live forms): editing `seon.lib/defc`'s body moved 298 digests, 0 before (each a stale green); :form/refs 28,344 to 33,916 datoms; import about 8 s either way; digests 602 to 705 ms. Not done, filed as gaps in docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach: a quoted name whose var does not exist at analysis (an imported test file quoting its own later var); `seon.program/reaching-tests` still follows refs only (40 tests for the defc edit against 298 digests), the selection design step 1 replaces; bin/test imports no schema resources and keeps them in :env, since resources/seon/schemas also declares the store's attributes every test uses."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.program/definitions-from-the-compiler"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-quoted-or-syntax-quoted-var-is-a-ref
                      seon.program-test/a-macro-use-is-a-ref
                      seon.program-test/a-test-reaching-a-quoted-function-changes-digest-with-it
                      seon.program-test/a-changed-macro-moves-the-digest-of-each-test-that-expands-it
                      seon.program-test/a-changed-contract-schema-moves-the-digest-of-tests-reaching-the-contracted-var}}
 {:seon.task/name "compiled-units-in-the-store"
  :seon.task/plan "Deferred, kept open (owner, 2026-10-22, notes/DECISIONS.md: \"Agreed: don't store compiled units now; forks already inherit compiled code\", and \"keep the idea open\"): not to be released until reopened; its proof is what reopening must make green. The numbers (worktree session store-native, notes/store-native-2026-10-22.md#q1): nothing compiles at an agent's fork (copy-on-write from the fork server, fork-ms p50 0.68 ms) or in a root boot beyond `seon.program/load-all!`'s catch-up of forms the binary lacks; a real form loads in 1.6-5 ms, of which ~3.6 ms is Jolt's analysis and emit plus Chez's compile (what a stored unit would replace, by a 0.01 ms `load-compiled-from-port` from bytes) and 1-2 ms contract arming (which stays). A Chez unit is deterministic bytes (100/100 identical across processes), 1.7-2.6 KB for a 450 B form; Jolt's AOT units are 15x their source (15.2 MB for Seon's 55 namespaces, 127 absolute paths in seon.lib's), and every Jolt or Chez pin bump strands them. Reopen when point-in-time loading is measured as the bottleneck: per-form units under (form text, what it reaches, runtime fingerprint: seon.db/each-commit-names-its-runtime) as values out of line, after Jolt emits one form to a bytevector with no absolute paths. The larger measured cost on that path is macro fan-out: `load!` of 67 seon.lib forms with their dependents took 2,367 ms against 109 ms for the forms alone (`seon.program/dependents`)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.db/each-commit-names-its-runtime"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-cost-test/loading-a-form-from-its-stored-unit-costs-its-arming}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-repl-at-a-commit"
  :seon.task/plan "THE PLAN A2 (a branch rebuilt from its definitions; evaluations are history). Already true (notes/store-native-2026-10-22.md#q3): `load-all!`, `load-changes!` and `load-diff!` read live forms' :form/src, and nothing re-runs an evaluation. What is missing is the function: a REPL on the program as of commit C, made by forking the fork server's image, `d/commit-as-db` at C (0.7-0.9 ms) and `load-diff!` to it (1.6-5 ms per differing form). Restores code and data defs exactly (main 2026-10-22: 1,119 code forms, 87 literal and 56 computed defs); host cells (7 atoms/delays) come back empty and host objects (ffi libraries, LMDB, sockets, procs, threads) are made fresh, as at boot; an agent's REPL state outside definitions stays with `clone!`. Waits for the owner's go on Q3."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.agent/an-agents-base-is-its-merge-base"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-repl-at-a-commit-holds-that-commits-program}}
 {:seon.task/name "loading-a-changed-file-keeps-its-other-vars-bound"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen by worktree session PROVE 2026-10-22: a root started on target/seon built from src, then src/seon/program.clj edited (one defn removed, `seon.program/changed-vars`; one docstring changed, `seon.program/reaching-tests`), with :seon.config.source/dirs [\"src\" \"test\"] (seon.agent-test/in-agent): in the agent (a fork of the fork server after the import), `(seon.program/reach-digests ...)` answers \"Attempting to call unbound fn: #'seon.program/reach-digests\", an unchanged var of the changed file, and `seon.agent/test-plan` (which calls it) selects nothing, so four seon.agent-test tests went red and one root hung in stop! (seon.e2e/await-quiet's watch saw the connection :released). Rebuilt from the same tree, all nine are green. So loading a changed file's forms into a process whose binary holds the file (`seon.program/load-changes!`, `seon.fork-server/server-step` :tx, after main's 9382656 runs every file's ns form first) leaves a var of that namespace it did not change unbound. Find which load does it (the ns form's evaluation, `seon.program/remove-definition!` of the removed var, or the load of the changed form), at the cause; a changed file loads its changed forms and leaves every other var as the binary holds it. Related: seon.source/an-import-reads-a-changed-ns-form-before-its-forms. Until fixed, bin/test builds target/seon before anything runs (docs/landing.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/loading-a-changed-file-keeps-its-other-vars-bound}}]
